From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toon Verwaest <verwaest@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:56:35 +0200
Subject: [parser] Fix error validation in object rest/spread properties

In `ParseProperty` (`case Token::kEllipsis`), when the operand of `...`
is not a valid reference expression (`!IsValidReferenceExpression`), it
can never be a valid rest binding or assignment target, because object
rest properties do not allow nested destructuring patterns.

Previously, `ParseProperty` did not call
`prop_info->accumulation_scope->ValidateExpression()` before recording
`kInvalidRestBindingPattern` and `kInvalidRestAssignmentPattern`. This
left inner expression errors unvalidated (so `({...{a = 42}} = {})` and
`({...{a = 42}}) => {}` reported outer rest pattern errors instead of
`Invalid shorthand property initializer`) and left inner pattern errors
uncleared in `scope_` (so `({...1} = {})` reported `Invalid
destructuring assignment target` instead of
`kInvalidRestAssignmentPattern`).

Additionally, `ParseProperty` checked `if (peek() != Token::kRightBrace)`
instead of `if (peek() == Token::kComma)` (unlike `ParseArrayLiteral`),
causing syntax errors like `let { ...a : 1 } = {}` to report `Rest
element must be last element` instead of `Unexpected token ':'`.

Validate the operand as an expression when it is not a valid reference,
and only record `kElementAfterRest` when followed by a comma.

